        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lafayette-la-princesse-de-cleves-b3b031f06d32fc7b\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"“Je vous aimais” is a direct, retrospective avowal that Monsieur de Clèves loved his wife, further specifying that his love made him willing to remain deceived. It strongly presents a past emotional state as true. It neither undertakes a new relational commitment nor functions independently as a reflex-like discharge; the emotional intensity belongs to the delivery and surrounding reproach.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The imperfect tense presents the love as past or no longer intact, consistent with his claim that she has taken away his esteem and tenderness; this temporal qualification does not alter the truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Monsieur de Clèves addresses “Je vous aimais” directly to Madame de Clèves during their deathbed confrontation, as represented in fictional dialogue.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal forms part of a sustained deathbed reproach. Monsieur de Clèves contrasts the depth and legitimacy of his former love with what he believes to be his wife's betrayal and with Nemours's allegedly seductive intentions. His jealousy and mistaken suspicions shape the accusation but do not change the core clause's status as an avowal of past love. Although the narration says his conflicting feelings could not remain contained, the highlighted clause itself is reflective and explanatory—“je l’avoue à ma honte”—rather than a reflex-like verbal eruption.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je vous aimais jusqu'à être bien aise d'être trompé, je l'avoue à ma honte.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit retrospective avowal and its elaboration strongly support truth-conditional force; “je l'avoue” presents it as a considered confession.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He says that she has taken from him “l'estime et la tendresse que j'avais pour vous” and later calls her “une personne que j'ai tant aimée.”\",\"supports\":\"The repeated past-tense descriptions corroborate that the highlighted utterance reports a former loving state rather than initiating a commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration says that his painful, conflicting feelings “ne les put renfermer en lui-même,” after which he delivers a lengthy reproach.\",\"supports\":\"This explains the emotional occasion and intensity of the speech, but the highlighted clause's reflective and argumentative role does not independently establish expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Adieu, Madame, vous regretterez quelque jour un homme qui vous aimait d'une passion véritable et légitime.”\",\"supports\":\"His farewell explicitly characterizes his love as genuine and legitimate while maintaining retrospective, declarative force rather than undertaking a future obligation.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core retrospective avowal. The deathbed emotion, jealousy, accusation, and marital setting are adequately handled as context and create no residual pressure requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
